# Answer 10 · The NGO-isation of the Women's Movement in Pakistan (CSS 2026)
@py question 2026-6

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define the terms, show how NGO-isation happened, apply the WAD critique, weigh both sides with Pakistani evidence, and reach a qualified verdict.
@ask CRITICALLY EXAMINE the "NGO-IZATION" of the women's movement in PAKISTAN
@ask Has DONOR-DRIVEN GENDER MAINSTREAMING DEPOLITICIZED the struggle? Use the WAD CRITIQUE
(1) Introduction: NGO-isation turns a movement into funded projects; the charge is depoliticisation
(2) Key terms: NGO-isation, depoliticisation, gender mainstreaming and WAD, explained simply
(3) How it happened: military rule, the Beijing process, donor money and state preference for services
(4) The WAD lens: women were always in development, on unequal terms; projects leave structures intact
(5) Evidence of depoliticisation: donor agendas, projectisation, professionalisation, upward accountability
(6) More evidence: urban and class bias, the "Western agenda" label, competition among NGOs
(7) Gender mainstreaming as technocracy: gender units, GRAPs and checklists that fade with funding
(8) The counter-case: NGOs researched and drafted the laws of 2006–2016 and kept space open
(9) More counter-case: services save lives; WAF stayed unfunded; the self-funded Aurat March
(10) Verdict: partly depoliticised, then re-politicised; a division of labour between NGOs and movements
(11) Conclusion: a movement needs both expertise and mobilisation, accountable to women
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Critically examine"** asks for **both sides** and a **reasoned verdict**, not agreement with the question's claim. Explain each loaded term — **NGO-ization**, **donor-driven**, **gender mainstreaming**, **depoliticized**, **technocratic project**, **social revolution** — and use the **WAD (Women and Development)** critique **explicitly**, as instructed. Pakistani evidence must support every point.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**NGO-isation** is the process by which a social movement's energy moves into **professional, formally registered, donor-funded non-governmental organisations**. Activists become paid staff, campaigns become **projects**, and accountability runs **upwards to donors** rather than **outwards to members**.

#### ii) Scholars
The idea was developed by **Sonia Alvarez** (on Latin America, ==1999==) and **Islah Jad** (on the Arab world, ==2004==).

#### iii) The argument
NGO-isation **partly** depoliticised Pakistan's women's movement, as the WAD critique predicts. But NGOs also produced laws, data and services, and the movement has **re-politicised** itself. The verdict is mixed.

### 2. The key terms

#### i) Depoliticisation
Turning a struggle about **power** — who owns, who rules, who decides — into a **technical** problem managed by experts. The question contrasts a "**technocratic project**" with a "**social revolution**".

#### ii) Gender mainstreaming
Endorsed at **Beijing** (==1995==): assessing every policy for its effects on women and men, instead of confining women to separate projects.

#### iii) WAD — Women and Development
A late-1970s critique, drawing on **Marxist and dependency theory**, of **WID** (Women in Development). WID said women were **left out** of development and should be integrated. WAD replied that women were **always** part of development — but on **unequal, exploitative terms** set by class and global capitalism.

### 3. How NGO-isation happened in Pakistan

#### i) Early organisations
**Shirkat Gah** (==1975==) began as a research and legal-rights collective. **WAF** (==1981==) was a voluntary platform that deliberately refused funding.

#### ii) The drivers
Military rule closed political space in the 1980s, so activists turned to organisations. After **Beijing** (==1995==) and **CEDAW** (==1996==), donors funded "gender and development" projects; hundreds of NGOs formed. NGOs offered jobs and some protection, and governments welcomed their **service delivery**.

#### iii) Growth and regulation
The 2000s brought donor-funded programmes on women's political participation and violence. From ==2015==, tighter state regulation of foreign-funded NGOs reduced this flow.

### 4. The WAD lens applied

#### i) Integration into an unequal system
Many projects offered women **micro-credit, handicraft training or income generation**. From the WAD standpoint, these integrate women into the existing economy without changing **land ownership, feudal power, wage exploitation or unpaid care**.

#### ii) An example
A home-based embroiderer trained by a project still sells to a middleman at piece rates and still does all the housework. Her income may rise slightly; her **position** does not change. WAD would ask instead who owns the land she works, who sets the piece rate, and who does the unpaid care that fills her day — questions that three-year projects rarely touch. This is the "project rather than revolution" the question describes.

::: fig cycle | How a movement becomes a project, as the critics describe it.
@centre A movement becomes a project
Donor priorities: themes set abroad
Funding cycles: three-year projects with targets
Professional staff: activists become employees
Upward reports: accountability to donors
Beneficiaries: women served, not organised
Less mobilisation: the street empties
:::

### 5. Evidence of depoliticisation (1)

#### i) Donor-driven agendas
Priorities followed donor fashions — micro-credit, then governance, then violence — rather than the demands of women themselves, and changed whenever a funding cycle ended.

#### ii) Projectisation
Three-year projects with log-frames and "deliverables" cannot sustain long struggles against feudal or patriarchal power.

#### iii) Professionalisation
Paid experts and **gender consultants** replaced volunteers; English-language reports replaced mass mobilisation.

#### iv) Upward accountability
NGOs answered to donors and their evaluations, not to the women they served.

### 6. Evidence of depoliticisation (2)

#### i) Urban and class bias
Offices clustered in Islamabad, Lahore and Karachi; rural women became "**beneficiaries**" rather than leaders.

#### ii) The legitimacy cost
Foreign funding let opponents dismiss feminism as a "**Western agenda**".

#### iii) Competition
NGOs competed for grants instead of building one movement, and some rural women came to see NGOs as **employers or service providers** rather than allies in a shared struggle.

### 7. Gender mainstreaming as technocracy
After Beijing, Pakistan adopted a **National Plan of Action** (==1998==), the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) and donor-supported **Gender Reform Action Plans**. They created gender units and gender-budgeting pilots — but critics describe many of these as **checklist exercises** run by consultants, understaffed and fading when funding ended. "Gender" often meant a **training workshop** rather than a shift of power.

### 8. The counter-case (1): what NGOs achieved

#### i) Laws
NGOs such as **Shirkat Gah** and the **Aurat Foundation**, working with women legislators, researched, drafted and lobbied for the **Protection of Women Act 2006** and the laws on **harassment** (==2010==), **acid attacks** (==2011==) and **honour killing** (==2016==). Lawmaking is deeply political.

#### ii) Space under repression
When parties were banned or weak, NGOs kept women's issues alive and trained women councillors after the ==2001== local-government reforms. They also built the **data** on violence and women's work — from newspaper monitoring to legal case records — without which Parliament could not have acted.

### 9. The counter-case (2): services and revival

#### i) Services save lives
Shelters, legal aid and helplines are not "depoliticised" to a woman escaping violence. Helping one woman leave an abusive home, or win her inheritance in court, changes power relations in that family — the personal is political.

#### ii) Not all NGOs are alike
**WAF** stayed unfunded and independent; many NGOs combine services with advocacy.

#### iii) The movement revived
The self-funded, volunteer-run **Aurat March** (from ==2018==) shows that grassroots feminism survived NGO-isation — and rural movements such as the **Sindhiani Tehreek** never depended on donors.

::: fig compare | A social movement and an NGO project.
## Social movement
- Goal: change power relations
- Members and volunteers
- Accountable to members
- Long-term and open-ended
- Protest, organising, politics
## NGO project
- Goal: measurable outputs
- Paid, professional staff
- Accountable to donors
- Short-term, fixed budget
- Training, services, advocacy
:::

### 10. The verdict

#### i) Partly true
The question's claim is **partly** true. Donor agendas, projectisation and professionalisation weakened mass mobilisation and made much gender work technocratic — as the **WAD** critique predicts.

#### ii) But incomplete
NGOs also produced **laws, data and services**, and the movement has **re-politicised** itself through WAF's legacy, rural struggles and the Aurat March.

#### iii) The way forward
A **division of labour**: NGOs for expertise, research and services; independent movements for mobilisation and politics. Both need **local funding** and **accountability to women themselves**.

### 11. Conclusion
NGO-isation turned parts of Pakistan's women's movement into a technocratic "project", as its critics and the WAD lens suggest. Yet it also helped win the laws of 2006–2016 and never fully replaced grassroots activism. The movement's challenge is not to choose between NGOs and revolution, but to make **expertise serve mobilisation** — so that gender work once again changes **power**, not only programmes.

## Why this answer scores
- Every loaded term is **defined**, and the **WAD critique** is applied explicitly with an example.
- **Both sides** are argued with Pakistani evidence, as "critically examine" requires.
- A **cycle figure** and a **compare figure** make the argument visible.
- The verdict is **qualified and reasoned**, with a practical way forward.
